```

Hmm, but if a subdirectory can't be listed, `copy` pushes it to skipped and returns false; the parent should then not keep the (empty) target dir. Simpler: create the target dir only after listing has succeeded. Then an unlistable subdir leaves nothing behind, and the parent's catch-all doesn't apply since copy returned false but didn't throw.

Let me rewrite:

```ts
const copy = (from: string, to: string): boolean => {
  let entries;
  try {
    entries = readdirSync(from, { withFileTypes: true });
  } catch {
    skipped.push(relative(source, from) || ".");
    return false;
  }
  mkdirSync(to, { recursive: true });
  chmodSync(to, DIRECTORY_MODE);
  for (const entry of entries) {
    ...
    if (entry.isDirectory()) copy(sourcePath, targetPath);  // its own failure is recorded there
    else ...
  }
  return true;
};

if (!copy(source, destination)) return undefined;
return skipped;
```

Wait — when `from === source` and it can't be listed, skipped gets "." and returning undefined. Fine; skipped is discarded. Good.

That's cleaner. Let me apply.

Also: relative(source, from) returns "" when from === source. Then `"" || "."` → ".". Good.

One more: `if (entry.name === ".git") continue;` — but `entry.isDirectory()` check ordering fine.

Also the parent copy's try/catch wraps copy(sourcePath, targetPath) for a directory — that catches a throw from copy's own mkdir/chmod, not the internal failure (which returns false). Fine.

